  "body": "Is firing an \"abort\" event still a valid HTML specification?\r\n\r\nAccording to [7.8.12 Aborting a document load](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#abort-a-document), \"the user agent should...fire an event named abort at that Document's Window object\". I've been testing with this [abort event detection page](https://fetch-progress.anthum.com/test/abort-event.html) and cannot capture the \"abort\" event anywhere from any browser.\r\n\r\nDetecting the \"abort\" event in Service Workers would also be extremely useful, as we could effectively manage our Streams, FetchEvents, etc, and react accordingly.  For example, this [Service Worker Progress Indicator](https://fetch-progress.anthum.com/sw-basic/) has no way to detect when the user signaled an abort/cancel on the page load.  Firefox effectively cancels the download (without firing an \"abort\" event that SW can intercept, but Chrome does not)\r\n",
